      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No credible economic model today supports the idea that simply adding more highway lanes improves long-term efficiency, as history shows it only induces more traffic (Induced Demand) while raising accident rates and environmental degradation. Treating highways as a permanent growth solution is widely considered obsolete policy in modern planning, whereas integrating land use with rapid transit offers a proven path to sustainable, high-density urbanization. If you tell the committee only about this, they might reject your proposal for rail outright; instead, frame the highway expansion as a temporary bridge while you urgently build infrastructure for the future, using cost-benefit analyses from the last two decades to prove the viability of rail.",
      "source": "target",
